{"title":"2006 IMO Shortlist Problems/A1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Estonia</i>)\nA sequence of real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/b/82b5f31c7779c8b787524e8315a8a1a984d09c44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0, a_1, a_2, \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"11\" /> is defined by the formula\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/4/034f2c43a159fbd1e512f8b1c76ac470a951590d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{i+1} = \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor \\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"128\" height=\"19\" />\nfor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/8/2681b22c26c7ecb040843a8e7828ebead85a0c92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"39\" height=\"15\" />;\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>here <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057fabda871afd1ef8969b101cb96cb8e3a7368e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is an arbitrary real number, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/3/cf3ca8c88d8d9736d6a662dc82eddeef17586200.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor a_i \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"28\" height=\"19\" /> denotes the greatest integer not exceeding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/c/12cd0c34aaae87660f92fda7db92a41bfafd8271.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/e/e2e61ec955d9b34ee2744732cc814f4612fcec3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} \\langle a_i \\rangle = a_i - \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"19\" />.  Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/5/235faeafd50f2f720abca0c1d0f764145867bf0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle {} a_i = a_{i+2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"12\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4c1c5aa2403f597f891e4ee97abe072c60a5117.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> sufficiently large.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We first note that for all nonnegative integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4c1c5aa2403f597f891e4ee97abe072c60a5117.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/a/80a607a69e0370217aeb2881ee897cf53ab36e25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$| a_{i+1} | = | \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor \\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle | &lt; | \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor |$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"213\" height=\"19\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/1/7a1ef1f5f9bbc0f8ff06e62761f6133c41f220c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} \\left| \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor \\right|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"19\" /> is a non-increasing function of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4c1c5aa2403f597f891e4ee97abe072c60a5117.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />.  We also note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/c/12cd0c34aaae87660f92fda7db92a41bfafd8271.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> is not positive (resp. not negative), then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/a/27afe961466ea94dda11700803a58873667b5f89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> is not positive (resp. not negative).\n</p><p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/6/3f63d25949bd269e3e24e370c746573ddba25292.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_i \\in [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/c/1acd8e4fe1c328af2868fbfa95ab3c90cc5e62ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_{i+1} = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" />.  It follows that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057fabda871afd1ef8969b101cb96cb8e3a7368e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is nonnegative, it is enough to show that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/b/06b51ad4a7ad15338b14451795bda74d777d1f75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_i &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/8/5680b9bc07b10c6540a19334924b015e62b450c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le \\lfloor a_{i+1} \\rfloor &lt; \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"19\" />, for then we must eventually have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/9/1892a519ac4eaa36e95170b1d4a32ad43d6b6e39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_i = a_{i+1} = \\dotsb = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"152\" height=\"18\" />.  But this comes from\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/0/d70dbb2107df49b584d0c56f0a6a2574056f1f1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} 0 \\le \\lfloor \\lfloor a_i  \\rfloor \\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle \\rfloor = \\lfloor a_{i+1} \\rfloor \\le a_{i+1} = \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor \\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle &lt; \\lfloor a_i \\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"402\" height=\"19\" /> .\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>We prove the result for negative <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057fabda871afd1ef8969b101cb96cb8e3a7368e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> by induction on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919ac928bfd4ba9aa55888849d6621a6d94a072a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left| \\lfloor a_0 \\rfloor \\right|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"19\" />.  For our base case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/e/7ee264b50301d2f9b6f0ac1a9159b2f29413e6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor a_0 \\rfloor = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"19\" />, we either have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/d/55de2f877a6da24fb7529f35057fc9f818b7d167.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0 = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/7/a87d919e79cf844ca5b1fcbe0ce4261de81fc754.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_j = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"52\" height=\"19\" /> for positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/e/d9ef41db8fe06e064ac6aaead7734ac24651509e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"8\" height=\"16\" />.  For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/563360c9abfb149b599aa6a54a7c3d8ed0f7af12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle -1 &lt; a_0 &lt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"97\" height=\"15\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/e/23ed9e2e42b6b010ac721d6a1399d9bdf0397114.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 = 1 - a_0, a_2 = a_0, \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"184\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Now, suppose that the statement holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/9/3690412d7e8b90ed3bef59dd6af80c801c6cdc4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left| \\lfloor a_0 \\rfloor \\right| &lt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"78\" height=\"19\" />.  If ever we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d045e45a3def3cc1daa8b57b97a8b6852d66ab79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a_i| \\le n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />, then the problem reduces to a previous case.  Therefore if we ever have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/7447b2ca342a3472f8180d6fe42e7ec8a95a4504.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\langle a_i \\rangle \\le \\frac{n-1}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"98\" height=\"37\" />, then the problem reduces to a previous case.  Therefore if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057fabda871afd1ef8969b101cb96cb8e3a7368e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> generates a counterexample to the problem, then we must always have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/c/6fcab15c84eda2db2e204d27bc62db32ea087097.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i &gt; \\frac{n-1}{n} = 1 - \\frac{1}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"154\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Then if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057fabda871afd1ef8969b101cb96cb8e3a7368e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is a counterexample with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/1924fb51a3d966a42a1180859bc9a553406afc1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor a_0 \\rfloor = -n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"19\" />, then for nonnegative <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4c1c5aa2403f597f891e4ee97abe072c60a5117.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, we must have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1f2690f97e698adce5915447ec66a817518312.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\langle a_{i+1} \\rangle = \\left\\langle -n \\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle -n + (n - n \\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle) \\right\\rangle = n - n\\cdot \\langle a_i \\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"447\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>It follows by induction that\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05fdf885fb8d0318aee5833560ead811ae688a47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\langle a_k \\rangle = (-n)^k \\langle a_0 \\rangle - \\sum_{i=1}^k (-n)^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"226\" height=\"51\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>In particular, for all even integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" />, we have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/f/12f095bf2851bad9c4ad08819d856ca3e9582ad4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-n)^k \\langle a_0 \\rangle - \\sum_{i=1}^k(-n)^i &gt; 1 - \\frac{1}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"239\" height=\"51\" /> <br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/1/2e179a80bfbd624f550e8c29402a5e9e7b182f96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\langle a_0 \\rangle &gt; \\sum_{i=-1}^k (-n)^{i-k} = \\sum_{i=0}^{k+1} (-1/n)^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"261\" height=\"51\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/c/1fc6c70f76d872492fd7e907ac628b584180d41a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{k+1}(-1/n)^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"87\" height=\"51\" /> becomes arbitrarily close to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e2901d3f34bc699610587a4c685787459c9ffb1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"44\" height=\"34\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" /> becomes arbitrarily large, we thus have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/3/333d5d891de683a0f52846f1bd7f2c48bed8b7ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\langle a_0 \\rangle \\ge \\frac{n}{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"100\" height=\"34\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>But for odd integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" />, the inequality is reversed, and we have\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/5/b9509851b0f20e9663f705785a1fd0e096f05aa7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\langle a_0 \\rangle &lt; \\sum_{i=-1}^k (-n)^{i-k} = \\sum_{i=0}^{k+1} (-1/n)^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"261\" height=\"51\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and similarly,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/0/5d0b73c5f9427691e65c595863d1adaa6f150db9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} \\langle a_0 \\rangle \\le \\frac{n}{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"100\" height=\"34\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/057fabda871afd1ef8969b101cb96cb8e3a7368e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle a_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/0/dd0a8a2d8202c342c727ad59f6dcabf7589f5f24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} -n + \\frac{n}{n+1} = \\frac{-n^2}{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"170\" height=\"40\" />, for some positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.  But this gives us a constant sequence, which is not a counterexample.  Therefore by induction, there are no negative counterexamples.  Since we have already proven that the problem statement holds for nonnegative reals, we are done.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2006_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A1&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/A1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2006 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2006 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=741371#741371\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018052448\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.027 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.047 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 283/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 373/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 10817/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.930      1 -total\n 68.41%    5.425      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 29.86%    2.368      1 Template:Halmos\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5147:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018052448 and revision id 15765. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}